~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] What this step does
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Sanity check that the model really learned chemistry and did not just memorise noise or benefit
  from a data leak.
  Procedure (Y-randomization): the measured potencies (pIC50) are randomly shuffled between
  molecules, which destroys any         true structure-activity relationship, and the same model
  is trained and cross-validated again. This is repeated many         times. A healthy model
  scores well on the real data and about R2 = 0 (or negative) on the shuffled data.
  R2 is the fraction of the variation in potency that the model explains: 1.0 is perfect, 0 is no
  better than always         predicting the average potency, negative is worse than that.
